> I'm afraid I still don't understand the motivation for this issue. Who are the expected consumers of this information? Why can't they read the resources from the spec?

@tallclair This change is required to make things simpler for o11y/monitoring/reporting tools.

They are currently reading from the spec, and that works today because a running pod is assumed to have acquired all the resources in requested after successful `canAdmitPod`.

My pod API change breaks that as CPU/memory spec may no longer reflect actual config. So we want o11y tools to switch over to reading actual resources from podStatus. Without this, they will have to do stuff like
```go
if resourceType == ResourceCPU || resourceType == ResourceMemory {
    readStatus(...)
} else {
    readSpec(...)
}
```

And if we add support for mutating other resource types in the future, tools will have to start closely tracking which release has what resources in podStatus to read from. It can get ugly very fast.

